Title: SPARK
Episode: 1 — Is It a Good Life?
Credit: Written by
Author: Kfir Andre Sasson
Draft date: 2026-11-14

# EPISODE 1 — Is It a Good Life?

## COLD OPEN

EXT. CITY INTERSECTION - NIGHT #E01-001#

= Universe B (unrevealed). Mike's second death, seen from a distance: a hook that hides whether the crash was an accident or a choice.

[[Never show Mike inside the car or at the moment of impact. Keep the crash ambiguous (accident vs. choice is an open question). Katie B must NOT appear.]]

A quiet intersection. Wet asphalt reflects the traffic lights.

A sleek, expensive black sedan glides into the intersection.

From the side, headlights. A car slams into it.

Glass bursts. Metal folds. The sedan spins and stops against a light pole.

Silence. A single hubcap rolls across the road and wobbles to a stop.

Distant sirens rise. Red and blue light washes over the wreck as a police cruiser and an ambulance pull in at the edge of frame.

[[Narration is delivered slowly, over the wreck, AFTER the crash and BEFORE the camera rises. Tone: frustrated, yet at peace with death.]]

MIKE (V.O.)
(slow, quiet)
See this wrecked car?
That's me in there.
(long beat)
Last time, I fought it. Every single second.
(beat)
This time... this time I just let go.
(beat)
Not sure if that makes me brave, or the biggest idiot alive.
(beat)
Well. Not really alive.
(beat)
This is the second time I've died.
(beat)
Yeah, the second time!
(beat)
I don't really believe it myself...
(long beat)
Okay. Enough with the whining. Let me tell you about the people I left behind. The people I love. Both times.

The CAMERA drifts up, away from the wreck, past the streetlights, into the night sky.

The sky starts to glow, a deep ember blue, as if lit from the inside.

.THE SPIRITUAL DIMENSION #E01-002#

= The spark returns to its soul: the film's mythology, shown before it is ever explained.

[[Visual language TBD in bible/locations/spiritual-dimension.md. Abstract energy, no faces: AI-friendly. Do NOT show where Mike's spark goes next (protects the Universe C twist).]]

A vast living web of light. Countless souls, each a glowing core of blue electricity, all connected by threads of current.

Sparks stream out of every soul and dive down into the dark. Others stream back up and sink into their souls.

One spark races upward through the current, toward its soul.

The spark hits its soul.

BOOM!

> SMASH TO BLACK.

Silence. Two seconds.

A single blue spark flickers in the dark. It flares, and the title burns in, ember blue:

> SPARK <

Beneath it, smaller, in plain white:

> Episode 1: Is It a Good Life? <

Both fade out like a dying ember.

[[Title card ~4 s total: SPARK ~1.5 s alone, then the episode name joins. Sound: faint electric crackle, cut dead into the "Level complete!" jingle.]]

[[Sound bridge: out of the black, a cheerful mobile-game jingle ("Level complete!") leads into Mike at his boring job in Universe A.]]

INT. HALLORAN SYSTEMS - OPEN OFFICE - DAY #E01-003#

= Universe A. Mike, sharp but bored, coasts through a job beneath him. His boss can't fault his work, only his attitude.

[[Opens on an ECU from in front of Mike: his hands and the phone screen. The game is a fictional match-3 (no real game IP). The "Level complete!" jingle from the cold open is this game.]]

A phone screen. A grid of glossy, candy-colored gems. LEVEL COMPLETE! flashes and fades. Next level.

Two thumbs fly across the glass. Swipe. Swipe. A chain of gems explodes in a shower of light.

JEFF (O.S.)
Mike.

The thumbs don't stop.

JEFF (O.S.)
Mike...

Swipe. Three gems line up. Almost...

JEFF (O.S.)
MIKE!

MIKE DAVIS (35) jolts in his chair. Behind him, arms crossed, stands JEFF (50s), his boss, face tight with frustration.

Mike drops the phone face-down on the desk and spins around.

MIKE
Jeff! Hey. What's up, man?

JEFF
What's up? You're asking me what's up? What's up is I've been standing here for three minutes. Three whole minutes... watching you play that stupid game.
(beat)
You're addicted. You need to seek help.
(beat)
But not during working hours, of course.

Jeff grins at his own joke. Mike doesn't. Jeff points at Mike's monitor. A screensaver drifts lazily across it.

MIKE
Really, Jeff?

JEFF
Yes, really. Even your computer gave up and went to sleep. When's the last time you touched this keyboard?

MIKE
(deadpan)
Define "touched."

Jeff's eyes narrow. Mike breaks into a grin.

MIKE
Kidding, I'm kidding. Don't worry, I'm done.

JEFF
Done with what?

MIKE
The finance module. Finished it this morning.

JEFF
Okay... if you say you finished, then show me.

Mike taps the mouse. The screensaver vanishes. A test dashboard: a long column of green. ALL TESTS PASSED.

MIKE
See? All tests are green. A hundred out of a hundred. You can also connect to the QA environment and check it out yourself.

Jeff leans in. Squints. Scrolls. Nothing to complain about.

JEFF
Huh.
(grudging)
...Okay. Fine.
(sighs)
Look. You know how much I appreciate you, right? When you actually work, nobody here comes close. That's what drives me nuts. Next time you finish something, you come tell me. Don't make me hunt you down. Okay?

MIKE
You got it, boss.

JEFF
Oh, and the deck for management. The new features thing. Don't forget.
(already walking away)
Thursday!

MIKE
Thursday. Thursday. I'm on it.

Jeff is gone. Mike turns back to his desk, picks up the phone, and leans back in his chair, completely at ease. His thumbs start moving again.

[[Hold on Mike playing, a few seconds. We don't see the screen, only his face, lit by it.]]

LIAM (30s), a coworker, leans over the cubicle wall.

LIAM
Lunch?

MIKE
Yeah, lunch. Let's go.

Mike pockets the phone and gets up. The two of them head out together.

INT. LUCA'S BISTRO - DAY #E01-004#

= Universe A. Over lunch, Mike admits he feels something is missing, but can't say what. Liam thinks he has it made. Olivia dares him to shake things up.

[[Quiet midday, mostly empty: no crowd. Three at one table: `multi-char` route, mostly singles and two-shots.]]

A small, half-empty bistro. Midday sun through the front window.

At a corner table, Mike and LIAM sit across from OLIVIA (25), bright and restless, a party wristband from last night still on her wrist.

Liam steals a fry off Mike's plate.

OLIVIA
So Jeff caught you playing your boring game again, huh?

MIKE
No. He caught me being done.

OLIVIA
You know the rest of us actually have to work, right?

MIKE
Says the girl still wearing her wristband from last night's party.

OLIVIA
It was an amazing party. At least the parts I remember. You should come next time.

MIKE
I think my party days are over.

Mike pushes his food around the plate.

MIKE
Can I tell you something stupid?
(beat)
I'm bored. Like... all the time.

LIAM
Bored.
(counting on his fingers)
Beautiful wife. Two great kids. A house. A job you can do in your sleep. Which you do.
(beat)
Man, you've got it made.

MIKE
I know. I know it is. That's what makes it stupid. Hard for me to explain.

He looks out the window.

MIKE
You ever get the feeling you missed something?
(beat)
Not a thing. Just... you know... something. Like you were supposed to be somewhere else.
(beat)
Does that make sense?

OLIVIA
Okay. So do something. Anything.
(counting, rapid-fire)
You could go skydiving. Surfing. Learn Japanese. Take a pottery class. I don't know. Anything!

LIAM
He'd get bored of pottery in a week.

OLIVIA
(to Mike, serious now)
Then find the thing you wouldn't get bored of.

Mike laughs it off. But he doesn't answer.

His phone buzzes on the table. He glances down.

The lock screen: Katie, Lucy and Joe, squeezed into one selfie, all of them laughing.

Mike looks at it a beat too long. A small smile.

[[Don't underline it: no music sting, no push-in. The answer to "Is It a Good Life?" is on the screen, and Mike doesn't see it yet.]]

INT. HALLORAN SYSTEMS - OPEN OFFICE - LATE AFTERNOON #E01-005#

= Universe A. The workday runs out like clockwork. Mike leaves on the dot, the same as every day.

Mike at his desk, working now. Fingers on the keyboard. On his monitor, charts and graphs shift as he types.

He clicks. Drags a chart into place.

He glances at the corner of the screen. 4:59 PM.

He watches it.

5:00 PM.

Mike saves, locks the computer, and stands in one motion. Grabs his jacket off the back of the chair and shrugs it on as he walks.

He threads between the desks toward the elevators.

MIKE
(to a coworker, passing)
Night, Sam.

SAM (O.S.)
Night, Mike.

MIKE
(to another, a wave)
See you tomorrow.

He passes Liam's cubicle. Liam looks up from his screen.

LIAM
That's it?

MIKE
Yeah. I'm done for the day.

LIAM
Alright. See you tomorrow, man.

MIKE
Bye, man. Take care.

The elevator is waiting, doors open. Mike steps in and turns around. He punches a button.

The doors slide shut on him.

INT. HALLORAN SYSTEMS - PARKING GARAGE - LEVEL 3 - EVENING #E01-006#

= Universe A. Mike can't find his car among rows of identical company sedans: his life as one more copy in a row.

[[Contrast to plant: in Universe B, Mike's car is the sleek black sedan from the cold open. Here, his car is one of a hundred.]]

The elevator doors open on a concrete garage.

Rows and rows of identical silver sedans. Same model. Same color. The same small company logo on every door.

Mike steps out. Stops. Looks left. Looks right.

MIKE
(to himself)
Where the hell did I park today?

He raises his key fob and presses it.

Nothing.

He heads right, down a row of silver cars, pressing the fob as he walks. Click. Click. Nothing.

He walks left, down another row that looks exactly the same. Click. Click. Nothing.

[[Edit as a jump cut between the two walks: same framing, mirrored direction. Comic rhythm.]]

He stops beside a concrete pillar. A big painted sign on it: P3.

Mike stares at it.

MIKE
Wait. Damn. I'm on the wrong floor.

INT. HALLORAN SYSTEMS - ELEVATOR - CONTINUOUS #E01-007#

Mike alone in the elevator. Hands in his pockets. The floor numbers tick by.

INT. HALLORAN SYSTEMS - PARKING GARAGE - LEVEL 4 - CONTINUOUS #E01-008#

The doors open. Another concrete floor. The same rows of identical silver sedans.

Mike steps out and presses the fob.

Far down a row, a car chirps. BEEP-BEEP. Its lights flash.

Mike walks to it, opens the door, and gets in.

INT. MIKE'S CAR - PARKING GARAGE - CONTINUOUS #E01-009#

= Universe A. Mike shakes off the day: the drive home, music up.

Mike drops into the driver's seat and pushes the start button. The dashboard lights up.

He clips his phone into the mount on the dashboard.

On the center screen, a map. He taps HOME. A blue route line draws itself across the city.

He taps again. Music kicks in: upbeat, driving, loud.

Mike nods along. He backs out of the spot and swings down the ramp, past row after row of identical silver sedans, out toward the evening light.

[[Music: original or licensed for commercial use (YouTube). Upbeat, to lift the pace after the slower office scenes.]]

INT. MIKE'S CAR - MOVING - EVENING #E01-010#

= Universe A. A near-miss at an intersection: a fake-out that makes us think we're watching the first death.

Mike drives through the city, music up. He sings under his breath along with it, drumming on the wheel. Easy. Calm. A small smile.

Streets slide by.

Up ahead, a traffic light turns red. Mike rolls to a stop at the line. Waits. Drums on the wheel.

EXT. CITY INTERSECTION - CONTINUOUS #E01-011#

[[Frame to rhyme with the cold open (#E01-001#): same angle, headlights from the side. The audience should think "this is it." It isn't. No music: street sound only.]]

The light turns green.

Mike's silver sedan eases forward into the intersection.

From the side: headlights, coming fast. A car racing to beat its own yellow.

Mike's brake lights flare. Tires shriek.

The sedan jerks to a stop, inches short. The other car tears across its bumper and is gone.

Silence.

INT. MIKE'S CAR - CONTINUOUS #E01-012#

The music, still playing, cheerful and oblivious.

Mike grips the wheel, white-knuckled, staring at the empty intersection.

MIKE
What the hell?!
(beat)
...Okay. Okay. I'm okay. I'm okay. Yeah... I'm okay.
(beat)
Holy shit, that was close. Wow.
(beat)
I could've died.

He exhales, long and shaky. Then a small, disbelieving laugh.

He turns the music down. Drives on, carefully.

EXT. SUBURBAN STREET - EVENING #E01-013#

Quiet streets. Trimmed lawns. Kids' bikes in the driveways.

Mike's silver sedan turns into a driveway and stops in front of a modest two-story house.

EXT. DAVIS HOUSE - DRIVEWAY - EVENING #E01-014#

Mike gets out of the car. He looks at the house for a beat, then walks up the path and reaches for the front door.

INT. DAVIS HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - EVENING #E01-015#

= Universe A. Home. Mike tries to reach his son, who's somewhere else entirely.

Mike comes in, drops his keys in a bowl by the door, and hangs his jacket on a hook.

On the couch, JOE DAVIS (10), headset on, controller in hand, leans into a video game on the TV. Explosions. Shouting teammates.

MIKE
Hey, buddy.

Nothing.

MIKE
Joe.

Nothing. Mike waves a hand at the edge of Joe's vision.

MIKE
Earth to Joe...

Mike steps in front of the TV.

Without taking his eyes off the screen, Joe leans around him and flicks a hand. Half "hi, Dad." Half "you're in the way."

Mike shakes his head, smiling, and steps aside.

INT. DAVIS HOUSE - KITCHEN - CONTINUOUS #E01-016#

= Universe A. The family at its warmest: dinner, a tiny accident, and the ritual of love, the film's emotional spine.

KATIE DAVIS (35) stands at the stove, stirring a pot. At the kitchen table, LUCY DAVIS (6) is bent over a drawing, crayons everywhere.

MIKE
Hi, girls!

LUCY
Daddy!

She drops her crayon, runs, and throws herself at his legs. He scoops her up, hugs her, and sets her down.

He crosses to Katie and kisses her.

MIKE
What's for dinner?

KATIE
Chicken and potatoes. And the bread's almost done. Smell that?

MIKE
(breathing it in)
Mmm. Yep, smells amazing.
(beat)
Want me to make the salad?

KATIE
Please. Be my guest. I'm going to throw in a load of laundry. Your underwear is waiting for me in there.
(handing him the dish towel)
Keep an eye on the food, okay?

Mike slings the towel over his shoulder. Katie heads out.

LUCY
Can I help?

MIKE
Sure you can, honey.

He sets a step stool at the counter. Lucy climbs up. He gives her a small cutting board, a little kid's knife, and a cucumber.

MIKE
Careful, okay? Do it slowly.

LUCY
I'll be careful, Daddy. Don't worry.

Mike turns back to the stove.

[[PILOT SEQUENCE: the text below is copied verbatim from script/pilot-test.fountain (#PILOT-001#) and is already in production. Fountain has no include, so it is duplicated: any change must be made in BOTH files.]]

A warm, lived-in kitchen. Golden evening light. Dinner in progress. Through the oven window, a loaf of bread is rising and turning golden.

MIKE DAVIS (35), dish towel over his shoulder, stirs a pot at the stove, humming a tune to himself.

LUCY DAVIS (6) stands on a step stool at the counter, slicing a cucumber with a small kid's knife. She smiles at her father's humming, tongue poking out in concentration.

Slice. Slice. Sli—

She yanks her hand back. A tiny bead of red on her fingertip.

Her lip trembles. Then the tears come, sudden and loud.

The humming stops. Mike turns from the stove. Two steps and he's beside her.

He crouches to her eye level and takes her hand. Looks at the finger. Barely a scratch. He kisses the fingertip.

Then he cups her wet cheeks in both hands.

MIKE
Lucy, don't cry. Do you know how much I love you? Do you?

LUCY
(sniffling)
How much, Daddy?

MIKE
Like the sun loves the sky.

Her crying slows. She leans in close.

LUCY
(whispering)
And will you always love me, Daddy?

MIKE
Always always!

LUCY
(whispering)
I love it when you tell me how much you love me.

MIKE
I know you do. I love it too.

She throws her arms around his neck and he hugs her tight.

Over Mike's shoulder, on Lucy's face: the tears still wet on her cheeks, and her smile slowly returns.

[[Production: opening = WS of the kitchen with an oven-window insert of the bread. The knife cut = ECU insert (fine hand action, keep it short). Ritual dialogue = `dialogue` route (InfiniteTalk). Crouch and hug = `performance` route (Wan-Animate-2 from phone video). Final shot = MCU on Lucy, frontal, over the back of Mike's shoulder. The humming tune must be original or public domain (licensing). Est. ~30–35 s.]]

[[END PILOT SEQUENCE]]

INT. DAVIS HOUSE - DINING NOOK - EVENING #E01-017#

= Universe A. Dinner: the family at ease. Everything Mike has, in one ordinary evening.

[[Four at one table is heavy for `multi-char`. Cover it with one establishing wide, then singles and two-shots.]]

The table is set. Mike, Joe and Lucy sit waiting. Lucy holds up her finger to admire it: a band-aid covered in little cartoon stars.

Katie comes in carrying the bread on a wooden board, still steaming. She sets it in the middle of the table and sits.

KATIE
Okay. Eat.

Hands everywhere at once.

KATIE
So. How was everyone's day?

LUCY
(holding up her finger)
I got hurt.

Katie looks at Mike.

MIKE
A scratch. A tiny scratch.

LUCY
There was blood.

MIKE
I wouldn't call it blood... One drop.

LUCY
(proudly)
I was very brave.

KATIE
I'm sure you were, sweetheart.
(to Joe)
Joe? School?

JOE
(mouth full)
Fine.

KATIE
Fine what?

JOE
Fine good.

MIKE
Wow. Don't overwhelm us with all of these details.

Joe grins despite himself.

JOE
Okay, I got an A on the science test.

MIKE
That's my boy!

He reaches over for a high five. Joe gives it, a little too cool for it.

KATIE
(to Mike)
And you?

MIKE
Honestly? Not my best day.
(beat)
Jeff caught me playing on my phone.

JOE
Busted.

KATIE
Again? Mike... Really?

MIKE
I was done! I'm not a robot, you know... Besides, this damn game... I can't stop.

LUCY
(scolding)
Hey! Language!

KATIE
(smiling, shaking her head)
Yes, Mike. Language!

MIKE
Sorry, sorry. It slipped.
(beat)
Anyway, and then, on the way home, some guy ran a red light. Came out of nowhere. Missed me by this much.

He holds two fingers an inch apart.

The table goes quiet. Katie puts down her fork.

KATIE
Were you hurt?

MIKE
No, no. I'm fine.
(beat)
I mean, I peed my pants a little. But other than that, I'm fine.

JOE
Dad! Gross!

The kids crack up. Katie laughs too, shaking her head, relieved.

LUCY
(holding up her finger)
But I got hurt.

MIKE
That's right, honey. You got hurt.
(beat)
And you were a lot braver than me.

Lucy nods, satisfied.

MIKE
(to Katie)
And how about you? How many strangers' mouths today?

KATIE
Twelve mouths and one of them actually bit me.

MIKE
(to the kids)
Your mother had her hands in twelve mouths today, and then she made us bread.

Lucy freezes mid-bite.

LUCY
...Did you wash them?

KATIE
(deadpan)
No. Dentists are only allowed to wash their hands once a week.

JOE
Ewww!

The whole table cracks up. Even Joe.

[[End on the wide: the four of them laughing, warm light. Hold a beat.]]

INT. DAVIS HOUSE - MASTER BEDROOM - NIGHT #E01-018#

= Universe A. Bedtime: the small, ordinary intimacy of a long marriage.

Katie sits on the edge of the bed in pajamas, brushing her hair.

Through the open bathroom door, Mike brushes his teeth.

MIKE
(mouth full of foam)
Dud you pack fuh the confuhrence yet?

KATIE
What?

Mike pulls the toothbrush out and leans into the doorway.

MIKE
(louder)
Did you pack for the conference yet?

KATIE
Not yet. I'll pack tomorrow.

Mike goes back to brushing. Spits. Rinses.

MIKE (O.S.)
So what's this one about?

KATIE
New technologies in dentistry. It's actually supposed to be really interesting. I hope...

Mike comes out, wiping his mouth with a towel.

MIKE
Let me guess. Robots. In the mouth.

KATIE
Kind of, yes.

MIKE
Nope. Never letting you near me again.

She throws a pillow at him. He catches it.

KATIE
Oh, and don't forget. Lucy's birthday is the day after I get back. So while I'm gone, you're on the cake, the balloons, and the goody bags.

MIKE
Goody bags?

KATIE
Fifteen of them.

MIKE
(flat)
Wonderful. Just wonderful.

KATIE
And she wants a unicorn cake. With a rainbow inside.

MIKE
Rainbow inside?

KATIE
You heard me.

MIKE
Six years old and already high-maintenance.

KATIE
Wonder where she gets it.

Mike sits down on the bed. Katie sets her brush on the nightstand.

KATIE
Give me a head start, okay? Let me fall asleep first. I can't take the snoring tonight.

MIKE
Sure, sure. Whatever you say.

She kisses him. They each lie back on their own side. Katie switches off the lamp.

Darkness. A beat.

Mike SNORES. Instantly. Loud.

Katie opens her eyes. Stares at the ceiling.

She reaches over and strokes his hair, part frustration, part tenderness.

.THE DREAM #E01-019#

= A nightmare: Mike's family says goodbye, until we realize they aren't leaving him. He's leaving them.

[[The audience must believe this is Mike's dream: cut straight in from Mike asleep (#E01-018#). Visual language: psychedelic and warm, the colors of the house bleeding and melting. NOT the blue electricity of the spiritual dimension.]]

A void of soft, warm light. Colors bleed like wet paint.

Katie, Joe and Lucy stand close together, the way they sat at dinner. Smiling.

Then, one by one, they start to wave goodbye.

Katie, with a sad, gentle smile.

Joe, with that same flick of the hand.

Lucy, a small wave of her band-aid finger.

MIKE (O.S.)
No... No! Don't go! Stay!

Mike reaches for them. His hand never gets closer.

MIKE
Don't go!

The camera pulls back, and back.

They haven't moved at all. It's Mike who's drifting, backward and upward, away from them. They grow smaller and smaller in the warm light, still waving.

MIKE
Please! Please!

Darkness.

INT. SARAH'S BEDROOM - NIGHT #E01-020#

[[Keep the first seconds dark and ambiguous: the audience should assume it's Mike waking up. Reveal only when the lamp comes on.]]

Darkness. A sharp GASP. A figure jolts upright in bed.

A trembling hand finds the lamp. Click.

It isn't Mike.

SARAH (52), a woman we've never seen, sits in a bed we've never seen, drenched in a cold sweat. Breathing hard.

She stares at nothing.

SARAH
(whispering)
Oh no.

> CUT TO BLACK.

> END OF EPISODE 1
